          "x_evidence": "Jobber was built for small businesses and remains primarily SMB-focused. Its pricing starts at $25/month and scales to teams. One Sacra analysis describes its prosumer/small-business positioning as deliberate. More than 100,000 customers, a mobile-first UX, and pricing accessible to solo operators all reflect SMB-native design. It has since added features appealing to slightly larger shops but has not pivoted enterprise. Scored on shipped surface.",
          "y_evidence": "Jobber's scalability ceiling is moderate. Third-party commentary notes that 'larger enterprises may outgrow its limits.' Its dashboards and reporting are described as basic by power users. Enterprise permissions and ERP depth are described as weak. The platform works for growing small-to-mid-size service businesses but is not positioned to support the 30+ location enterprise operators that ServiceTitan targets. Scored on shipped surface.",
          "x_evidence_source": "researched",
          "y_evidence_source": "researched"
        },
        {
          "competitor": "housecall-pro",
          "x": 68,
          "y": 60,
          "x_evidence": "Housecall Pro was built with the SMB owner-operator in mind. 94.7% of G2 reviewers are from companies with 50 or fewer employees. Its pricing, onboarding, and UX are calibrated for small field-service businesses. The product has added features since launch (AI, financing, marketing), some of which are more enterprise-adjacent, but its core design remains SMB-first. Slightly more SMB-native than Jobber in aggregate review sentiment (both share ~8.8–8.9 setup scores). Scored on shipped surface.",
          "y_evidence": "Housecall Pro claims it targets teams of 1 to 100+ and pitches scalability on its marketing site. However, independent reviews note that 'customization options are somewhat limited compared to enterprise-level solutions' and that some advanced features (sales proposals, recurring service plans, pipeline management) require extra fees. The platform is a strong mid-market tool but hits a natural ceiling before enterprise scale. Scored on shipped surface.",
          "x_evidence_source": "researched",
          "y_evidence_source": "researched"
        },
        {
          "competitor": "_startup",
          "x": 82,
          "y": 28,
          "x_evidence": "Tessivane's design is explicitly SMB-native: pricing described as 'accessible to SMBs without enterprise budgets,' UX designed 'for businesses without dedicated operations teams,' and a mobile-first technician experience aimed at 2–50 technician companies. It is the only competitor in the set that has not accumulated enterprise features through years of up-market drift. Scored on shipped surface: no independent benchmarks exist; score is calibrated against Jobber (65) and Housecall Pro (68) as the current SMB-focused incumbents, with Tessivane positioned marginally more SMB-native by design premise.",
          "y_evidence": "As a seed-stage startup shipping its core scheduling and dispatch module, Tessivane's scalability ceiling is low relative to established platforms. No multi-location features, enterprise reporting, advanced permissions, or accounting depth are described in the product profile. This is appropriate for its stage — the finding is about current shipped surface, not a design flaw. A seed-stage product focused on fast time-to-value has not yet built the feature depth needed to retain a 30-technician company that wants to grow to 100. Scored on shipped surface.",
          "x_evidence_source": "agent_estimate",
          "y_evidence_source": "agent_estimate"
        }
      ]
