  "differentiation_claims": [
    {
      "claim": "SMB-first simplicity: lighter-weight UX and pricing designed for smaller service businesses, not enterprise contractors",
      "verifiable": true,
      "evidence": "The claim is directionally verifiable on two dimensions. First, against ServiceTitan: independently researched evidence confirms ServiceTitan's onboarding is materially harder (G2 setup score 6.7 vs. 8.8–8.9 for SMB tools), and its pricing ($250–$398+/month) is out of reach for the smallest operators. Tessivane's positioning as lower-complexity and lower-cost is credible against that comparison. Second, against Jobber and Housecall Pro: both are already SMB-native (G2 setup scores 8.9 and 8.8 respectively, both under $300/month entry tiers), so the differentiating gap narrows significantly. Tessivane's claim holds clearly when the reference point is ServiceTitan but is harder to substantiate against the mid-tier SMB-focused incumbents.",
      "challenge": "Investors will note that Jobber and Housecall Pro already occupy the 'SMB-friendly field service software' position and have done so for years with large customer bases (100K+ for Jobber). The claim 'not enterprise contractors' is accurate but not distinctive — Jobber and Housecall Pro also position against enterprise complexity. The question investors will ask is: what does Tessivane do that Jobber does not, at comparable or lower price? Without a sharper differentiator than 'simpler,' the claim partially holds but does not clearly separate Tessivane from two well-funded incumbents who occupy the same stated position.",
      "verdict": "partially_holds"
    }
  ],
